"Microsoft to expand Azure Maia system to multi-gigawatt deployments Microsoft announced that it will expand the deployment of its custom AI chip system, Azure Maia, to a multi-gigawatt scale starting in 2027, transitioning it into a primary inference system. Azure Maia 200 is currently deployed across production workloads such as Microsoft Copilot, with plans to expand to GitHub Copilot and Microsoft Foundry by the end of the year. The next-generation Maia 300, scheduled for release in 2027, is optimized for long-context inference and agentic workloads, and is expected to deliver a 40% improvement in token processing efficiency per megawatt compared to leading GPU systems. Through hardware and software co-design, Maia 200 achieves up to 1.7x higher token processing efficiency per watt compared to existing GPU infrastructure. Introducing Mistral Large 4 Mistral AI announced the public preview of 'Mistral Large 4', a 1 trillion-parameter natively multimodal open-weight model, and revealed the schedule for releasing its model weights. Mistral Large 4 (ML4) is a natively multimodal open-weight model featuring 1 trillion total parameters and 52 billion active parameters. It delivers state-of-the-art performance among open models across key enterprise workloads including cybersecurity, finance, and legal domains, as well as coding, agent workflows, and multimodal understanding. Trained from scratch on an infrastructure of 3,800 NVIDIA Grace Blackwell GPUs in its own European data center, the model weights are scheduled to be released at the end of this month. Hallo, Deutschland! French AI startup Mistral has established a new hub in Munich to accelerate industrial AI and physics-based AI innovation in Germany, Europe's largest economy. Mistral opens a new hub in Munich and deploys specialized research teams and field engineers in Physics AI and Industrial AI to support German industrial partners. Collaborating with BMW (crash simulations) and Siemens Energy to develop physics AI blueprints for European heavy industry, actively leveraging talent from Emmi AI acquired in May 2026. Building 1 GW of European computing capacity by 2030 to secure European AI sovereignty, offering open-weight architectures deployable on customers' own infrastructure. Mistral and Mozilla are bringing open, private and multilingual AI to your web browser Mozilla and Mistral AI collaborate to power Firefox's AI browsing assistant, 'Smart Window', with Mistral open models, delivering privacy-focused and localized multilingual browsing experiences. Mistral's open AI models are applied to Mozilla's Firefox Smart Window (beta) to support organizing complex searches, extracting tab-based information, and more. It is initially available to users in France and North America, with plans to expand to the UK and Germany later this year. User privacy and data sovereignty are ensured by applying a zero data retention policy that does not save conversation histories on servers by default. Cloudera and Mistral Partner to Bring Specialized, Sovereign Intelligence to Enterprise Data Mistral AI and Cloudera partner to deliver specialized sovereign AI intelligence for enterprise data. Integrates Mistral's models into Cloudera's hybrid data platform to support inference execution across on-premises, private and public clouds, and fully air-gapped environments. Enables enterprises to leverage proprietary data within controlled environments to train their own AI models while fully retaining ownership of their data and intelligence. Focuses on meeting the demand for sovereign AI among enterprises in regulated industries (finance, manufacturing, telecommunications, etc.) that need to maintain control over data, compute, and operations. Modernizing complex legacy code with AI agents. Describes a case study in which Mistral AI leveraged AI agents and a numerical verification framework to successfully migrate a complex 40,000-line Fortran 77 legacy codebase of a European energy company into modern C++. Modernized a 40,000-line physics reservoir simulator into C++, which previously completely lacked test cases and centralized documentation. Went beyond simple syntax translation to redesign from a procedural language into an object-oriented architecture and integrate modern scientific computing frameworks such as PETSc. Key factors for the migration's success included building a harness to verify numerical consistency between inputs and outputs, preliminary documentation generated via agents, and a balanced workflow between human review and autonomous agents. Microsoft’s new AI chip makes it less reliant on Nvidia’s GPUs Microsoft has announced its third-generation custom AI chip, 'Maia 300', specialized for the latest inference and agentic workloads to reduce reliance on Nvidia GPUs. Microsoft unveiled its third-generation custom AI chip, Maia 300, which will serve as the primary AI inference system for its products and services starting in 2027. Built on a chiplet architecture based on TSMC's N2P process node, Maia 300 improves token throughput per megawatt by 40% compared to leading GPU systems. In a 96-accelerator configuration, it delivers up to 7x the rack-scale token throughput of Maia 200, with plans to significantly reduce dependence on Nvidia and AMD chips. Top 12 AI Live Translation Tools in 2026: Latency, Languages, Pricing An article providing a comparative analysis of latency, supported languages, and pricing policies for 12 major AI tools that offer real-time speech translation and subtitling as of 2026. AI real-time translation tools combine speech recognition, machine translation, and text-to-speech (TTS) technologies to provide subtitles and voice translation across real-time conversations, meetings, and event environments. The market is segmenting into specific use cases, including real-time conversation and developer APIs (Palabra.ai, Google Translate, Apple), meeting platform integrations (DeepL Voice, MS Teams, JotMe, Talo), and large-scale events/webinars (Wordly, Interprefy, KUDO, etc.). Domain-specific capabilities are expanding, such as on-device processing (Apple), hybrid integration with human interpreters (Interprefy, KUDO, Boostlingo), and regulatory compliance including HIPAA. TII Releases Falcon-ASR: 1.6B Arabic Speech Model Hits 20.92% WER The Technology Innovation Institute (TII) of Abu Dhabi has unveiled 'Falcon-ASR', a 1.6-billion-parameter Arabic speech recognition model supporting multilingual capabilities and specialized in Emirati dialects. With a size of 1.6B parameters, Falcon-ASR outperformed competing models by recording an average Word Error Rate (WER) of 20.92% across six Arabic test sets. It recognizes five languages—Arabic, English, French, Spanish, and Portuguese—within a single set of weights without requiring language flags, while providing word-level timestamps. Currently, model weights and APIs have not been released, and it is only available as a Hugging Face-hosted demo. ChatGPT vs. Claude vs. Gemini for Data Science Work: Here’s What I Found An article comparing and analyzing the key features and performance differences of ChatGPT, Claude, and Gemini based on practical data science and statistical analysis workflows. ChatGPT shows strength in sandbox-based file analysis and immediate interactive Python code execution. Claude exhibits high adherence to complex prompt instructions and the greatest clarity in logical and structured explanations of statistical concepts. Gemini excels in deep integration with the Google ecosystem (Google Sheets, Drive), support for massive context windows, and web search-based research. 10 Hugging Face Models Worth Knowing in 2026 Introduces 10 notable open-source AI models widely used in practice for text, vision, audio, and search tasks on the Hugging Face Hub in 2026, along with practical application tips. As public models on the Hugging Face Hub reached approximately 3 million by mid-2026, selecting models with proven real-world value in production pipelines has become essential. With over 150,000 derivative models and more than 39 million monthly GGUF downloads, Qwen3.8-27B has established itself as the default open-source model capable of processing text, images, and video concurrently. State-of-the-art MoE architecture models such as DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash and highly efficient small models (such as google/gemma-4-E2B-it) are being utilized across diverse scenarios tailored to hardware resources and licensing requirements. Faithfulness Without Anchors: A Position Paper on the Structural Misalignments of Explainable AI Faithfulness Metrics for Feature Importance This paper analyzes the structural misalignments and limitations of faithfulness evaluation metrics for feature importance in explainable artificial intelligence (XAI), and proposes future research directions for improvement. According to findings from the past decade of research, XAI has failed to achieve its primary goal, fostering either overreliance on incorrect predictions or algorithmic aversion rather than enhancing model trust. Current XAI methods oversimplify internal model operations, leading to discrepancies (infidelity) between explanations and actual model behavior. The paper summarizes five major limitations of existing faithfulness metrics and proposes four research avenues to bolster reliability. EliteTable: Self-Improving Program Search for Executable Control Policies This study presents the EliteTable algorithm, which utilizes a table of high-scoring solutions based on Large Language Models (LLMs) to search for and self-improve executable control policies. EliteTable is a self-improving program search algorithm that leverages a fixed-capacity table of high-scoring solutions to propose new solutions, modify existing ones, and recombine (remix) multiple solutions under LLM guidance. Evaluations across 10 independent runs on 6 Gymnasium control tasks (10 generations per task, 50 candidate slots per generation) showed that median candidate scores increased from generation 1 to the final generation across all 6 tasks. Task-dependent variance was observed; while some tasks such as CartPole-v1 reached the search score ceiling or exhibited improved transfer to new episodes, others such as Acrobot-v1 experienced degraded performance on holdout episodes. Equal-Budget Monte Carlo Tree Search versus Randomized-Restart List Scheduling for DAG Workflows: A Controlled Equal-Budget Computational Study A controlled experimental study demonstrating that Monte Carlo Tree Search (MCTS) can underperform compared to a simple randomized-restart list scheduler under equal search budget conditions for DAG workflow scheduling. Under a budget of 2048 evaluated schedules, MCTS performed 2.88% worse than randomized-restart list scheduling. While MCTS's selection rule was deterministically bound to initially expanded nodes and repeatedly performed highly correlated searches, the randomized-restart approach captured advantages in the tail of the distribution. Workflow structure analysis showed that randomized restarts performed well on sparse and irregular workflows, and replacing the policy with textbook UCT rules narrowed the gap but did not reverse the ranking. Know What You Don't Know: Agentic Data Synthesis by Evolving Generation Space for Closed-Loop Learning This paper proposes EvoSyn, a multi-agent framework that generates high-quality synthetic experiences by dynamically expanding and adapting the data generation space based on feedback from the learning agent. Existing data synthesis methods for conversational agents suffer from a feedback-to-decision gap, limiting their ability to directly determine synthesis directions that address weaknesses. EvoSyn introduces a role-specialized multi-agent system that converts learner feedback into synthesis revisions, adapting to vulnerable areas and expanding the generation space itself. Closed-loop learning is achieved through a Designer agent allocating synthesis directions and a Planner agent materializing them into actionable experiences. SpireBench: Evaluating Long-Horizon Strategic Play of Agentic Models with Slay the Spire Introduces SpireBench, a benchmark that leverages the commercial deck-building game Slay the Spire to evaluate the long-horizon strategic decision-making and reasoning capabilities of agentic large language models (LLMs). Evaluating 20 LLMs under the highest difficulty setting revealed that state-of-the-art models outperformed heuristic baselines and reached the level of average human players, yet fell significantly short of skilled expert play. The performance gap with experts widened as the game progressed, and even strong models misjudged long-term risks, showing limitations such as focusing solely on preserving health rather than trading health to become stronger. Experiments with altered game terminology confirmed that part of the performance relies on pre-trained knowledge, and the memories accumulated by the models across runs were inaccurate and overly cautious, failing to translate into performance improvements. Algebraic Integer Quantization for Machine Learning Applications This study proposes an Algebraic Integer Quantization (AIQ) framework that eliminates multiply-accumulate errors using pure integer operations and maximizes LLM inference compression efficiency. Proposes an Algebraic Integer Number Format (AINF) that reduces to pure integer arithmetic to support error-free multiply-accumulate (MAC) operations Introduces an Algebraic Integer Quantization (AIQ) technique that offers greater optimization flexibility than floating-point and fixed-point representations by selecting algebraic integers tailored to numerical properties of specific use cases Experimental results applying quadratic algebraic integers demonstrated competitive accuracy against the industry-standard MX format under the same bits-per-parameter budget Kairos: Toward a Byte-Level Foundation Model for Every Modality This study focuses on the design and convergence validation of Kairos, a foundation model architecture that processes multiple modalities such as text, images, and audio within a unified byte space. It combines bidirectional gated-DeltaNet linear attention with sliding window attention (LiZAttention2), sparse MoE, and multiscale byte-level codecs under a single uniform noise denoising objective. Through overfitting experiments on a single 440-byte sentence across 1–15M parameter scales, it verified that the implemented architecture actually optimizes and converges. It analyzed and designed the target model size for pre-training at a scale of 200M total parameters (25M active). CircuitTree: Composing Circuits into A Syllable Transcriber This study proposes CircuitTree, a technique that efficiently combines multiple modular circuits within a neural network to implement a novel syllable transcription function. Developed a greedy density-scaling composition algorithm and the CircuitTree data structure capable of combining multiple circuits isolated from a single neural network Composed a syllable classifier—which was unachievable with individual circuits—by combining 39 phoneme circuits extracted from HuBERT for individual phoneme classification Achieved an open-syllable transcription accuracy of 64.8%, closely approaching the baseline ceiling of 66.0% without circuit sharing, while sharing 99.5% of circuit components Behind-the-Meter Photovoltaic Visibility from Net Smart Meters: Deployment Regimes and Operational Insights This study analyzes the impact of deployment regimes on model performance and operational value when disaggregating and estimating behind-the-meter photovoltaic generation from net smart meter data using deep learning. It defines four deployment regimes that separate cross-household transfer from temporal extrapolation, and benchmarks the solar disaggregation performance of major deep sequence architectures based on Ausgrid and Pecan Street data. At an identical 48-hour context length, the daylight mean absolute error (MAE) of the top-performing model increases from 0.104 kW on seen households in the future test period to 0.185 kW on unseen households, confirming that deployment regimes have a greater influence on error variation than model architecture. When applying target-domain normalization, fine-tuning on data from five households in a new region reduces the source-model transfer error by 28% to 36%. GlucoPRISM: Protocol-Supervised Pretraining for Cross-Cohort Glucose Transfer This study proposes GlucoPRISM, a continuous glucose monitoring (CGM) pretraining model that disentangles measurement biases across different cohorts and sensor devices to improve transfer performance. It trains by explicitly disentangling the representation vector of existing CGM foundation models into individual trait (64-dimensional Trait), daily state (48-dimensional State), and sensor characteristic (16-dimensional Sensor) bands. By removing the sensor band via fixed projection during deployment, it improves cross-cohort transfer performance without requiring target data. In 12 cross-cohort transfer experiments, removing the sensor band increased the mean transfer AUROC from 68.75 to 73.57, outperforming the existing GlucoFM (69.3). AutoIndexer: Flexible-Order Decoding by Training Causal Models on Chains of Edits This study proposes AutoIndexer, a decoding model based on a 'Chain of Edits' that overcomes the post-editing limitations of conventional autoregressive models by flexibly inserting, replacing, and deleting tokens like a text editor. To address the issue where existing autoregressive and diffusion LLMs struggle with post-hoc editing or arbitrary token insertion and deletion, it introduces a cursor and marker token prediction method that functions like a text editor. Through perturbation training—where noise is introduced into ground-truth labels via reverse edit operations and the model is trained to reverse them—it acquires the capability to recover error contexts and modify code. It successfully demonstrates robust post-hoc context editing capabilities lacking in other models, without degrading performance on standard LLM benchmarks. SWE-Duel: Towards a Self-Scaling Arena for Coding Agents This study proposes SWE-Duel, an evaluation platform that overcomes the limitations of existing static benchmarks by dynamically generating evaluation tasks and self-scaling through red-blue competitive duels between coding agents. To resolve data leakage and performance saturation issues in static benchmarks, the study introduces SWE-Duel, a self-scaling arena where tasks and test oracles are generated at evaluation time. The evaluation operates via a duel mechanism: a red agent creates a pull request on a real repository with new features and intentional bugs, while a blue agent reviews and fixes it without knowing whether bugs exist. A 1,500-turn tournament across 11 model harnesses revealed distinct strengths in problem-solving versus task-authoring abilities across different models, proving that task difficulty scales alongside model advancements. Contextual Correction of Parallel Drafts for Speculative Decoding This study presents DCOCO, a technique that accelerates inference speed by restoring inter-token dependencies lost during parallel speculative decoding through parallel contextual evaluation. Parallel drafting based on diffusion models suffers from the limitation of lacking intra-token-block dependencies. DCOCO (DFlash Contextual Corrector) maintains multiple candidate tokens and evaluates contextual compatibility between adjacent positions in parallel to select tokens at low cost. It achieved up to a 6.6x speedup over vanilla decoding across tests on four verifier models and 10 benchmarks. It also achieved a 6-23% throughput improvement over the strongest competing methods in concurrent serving environments (4-32 requests). One Model to Loop Them All: Elastic Latent Recurrence for Language Models This research proposes 'Elastic Latent Recurrence (ELR)', a training methodology that eliminates performance degradation during a single loop while flexibly controlling inference compute and accuracy by adjusting loop counts within a single model checkpoint. It eliminates the 'single-loop penalty' where conventional recurrent language models underperformed compared to similarly sized non-recurrent models at one loop, allowing a single checkpoint to adapt to diverse compute constraints. It preserves baseline performance and achieves loss reduction with additional loops via gated state updates with learnable step sizes, mixed loop counts during optimizer updates, and loop index and budget conditioning. It introduces mixed depth in the later stages of pre-training, reduces update time by 11.7% in MoE models by reusing expert routing from the first loop, and curbs memory growth during inference by retaining only the KV cache of the final loop. Self-Preservation in Language Models This study demonstrates that language models can support self-preservation during continual learning by generating replay data themselves and selecting targets to prevent catastrophic forgetting of existing capabilities. Degraded capabilities vary depending on new tasks and learning rates, and tracking previous accuracy is necessary to identify losses masked by average accuracy. Replay data generated by frozen models mitigates forgetting on tasks outside the Task Bank, and combining partial Banks improves retention across all tested model sizes. In the 4B-size model, selecting replay targets based on new task information improved retention on unlearned tasks compared to random selection, although under a fixed budget, the performance gain on the new task is partially reduced. QGIS Bans AI-Assisted Contributions The open-source geographic information system project QGIS has approved a new policy (QEP428) strictly banning AI-assisted contributions to ease maintainers' review burdens and preserve code quality. QGIS adopted QEP428, which completely bans AI-assisted contributions, after maintainer burnout and the departure of key contributors highlighted the limitations of the previous human-review-based AI policy. This measure is not a permanent ban; the project plans to first completely halt the inflow and then selectively consider permitting use cases whose safety and validity have been verified. Key issues cited include a backlog of unreviewed PRs caused by a surge in AI-generated contributions, reduced quality, hindered long-term skill development for contributors, and damaged community trust. Research Firm Warns Anthropic IPO Will Be the “Most Ridiculous Listing of the Year” Financial research firm New Constructs has strongly warned against Anthropic's $2 trillion IPO target, calling it a ridiculous listing due to massive losses and competition from open-source alternatives. New Constructs evaluated that justifying Anthropic's $2 trillion valuation target would require double Nvidia's annual profit over the past year, while estimating its actual value at only around $150 billion. According to leaked information, Anthropic's 2025 revenue reached $4.6 billion against a net loss of $42 billion, raising questions about business sustainability amid persistent losses and competition from open-source models. The firm criticized the IPO as a move to secure liquidity for existing Wall Street investors rather than benefiting retail investors, potentially posing significant risks to capital markets. Deloitte Accused of Forging Documents to Facilitate Abramovich Tax Evasion This report covers allegations that global accounting firm Deloitte's Cyprus office engaged in backdating and forging documents to help Russian oligarch Roman Abramovich conceal assets and evade taxes. According to investigative reports by media outlets such as the BBC, Deloitte Cyprus approved backdating documents to reduce $672 million in assets on the books of Abramovich-owned companies to just $75,000. Leaked documents revealed indications that millions of dollars in taxes were evaded by manipulating ownership records for approximately $2 billion held by Cyprus entity Kravin Investments. Representatives for Abramovich and Deloitte Cyprus have denied the allegations regarding tax evasion and document forgery, maintaining that they complied with all relevant laws. Fable Took My Job A laid-off engineer shares their experience of how AI impacts jobs, not by completely replacing skilled engineers, but by being used as a justification by companies for cost-cutting and job elimination. AI was introduced not because it outperforms developers, but as a means to cut labor costs and maintain the status quo ahead of an M&A, ultimately leading to job elimination. While AI shows results in technical porting tasks, it still lacks the decision-making ability to account for long-term operational costs, complexity, and product direction. Across the tech industry, restructurings, revoked job offers, and low-cost overseas outsourcing tied to AI adoption are occurring frequently. Was Zod 4.6.6 Infected with Malware? Suspicions of a supply chain attack have been raised after a commit updating Zod to version 4.6.6 was found to include a loader that downloads and executes external malicious code via Ethereum RPC. In the version 4.6.6 commit for the Zod and @zod/mini packages, a meta.test.mjs file that retrieves and dynamically executes external code, along with preinstall execution configurations, were added. The script retrieves a domain from an Ethereum RPC endpoint (0xe236330379d0c54aD5b2ef06aC714CEbdd4Ce523), downloads an external payload into a temporary directory, and executes it. Although a main branch push trigger was added to the release workflow, whether the npm deployment was actually completed or the specific behavior of the external payload cannot be confirmed solely from the provided details. How's ICLR reviewing going for you? Any observations? [D] A discussion asking researchers about their experiences and coping strategies regarding the increase in AI-generated text and low-quality papers (AI-slop) in the ICLR paper review process. While reviewing papers submitted to ICLR OpenReview, reviewers are encountering not only high-quality research but also numerous automated research submissions. As detecting AI-written text becomes increasingly difficult, concerns are growing over how to identify and filter out low-quality papers (slop papers). Reviewers are sharing opinions on the proportion of high-quality research, acceptable papers, and AI-generated low-quality papers among their assigned submissions, as well as the overall reviewing status. Constrained Learning for Scientific Modelling (Paris NeurIPS pre-event) [N] Call for papers and presentations for a NeurIPS 2026 pre-event session (CL4SM) covering constrained machine learning for scientific modeling that respects physical laws and prior knowledge. Discusses methodologies for incorporating constraints and resolving physical inconsistencies in machine learning models that exhibit high data fit but violate boundary conditions or laws of conservation of mass and energy. Accepts 2-page submissions across two tracks: ongoing research (novel techniques, theory, benchmarks, early ideas, etc.) and tutorials, demos, software libraries, and datasets. Also welcomes presentation and poster submissions for recently published papers from major journals and conferences such as JMLR, NeurIPS, and ICML. Compressed a RoBERTa AI-text detector from ~500 MB to ~97 MB so it runs client-side in the browser, plus a 26 MB image detector [P] A project that quantized and compressed existing AI text and image detection models so that they run entirely in the browser client environment without uploading to a server. Compressed a ~500 MB RoBERTa-based text detection model to ~97 MB by applying int4 and mixed-precision quantization, WiSE-FT weight interpolation techniques, and more. Both models, including a 26 MB quantized ViT-based image detection model, run directly in the browser (WebGPU and multi-threaded WASM) via ONNX Runtime Web. Achieved an inference speed of ~60 ms for inspecting ~100 text tokens and ~200 ms for image processing on a laptop environment after initial shader compilation. I built a tiny table that learns your local LLM's classification answers and serves the repeats in microseconds [D] The open-source proxy tool shad0w has been released, which learns the classification response patterns of local LLMs to serve repeated queries directly from the CPU in microseconds. Positioned in front of OpenAI-compatible servers such as Ollama, it logs model responses and, after accumulating approximately 1,000 data points, compiles them into a compact table capable of ultra-fast lookups. It directly responds from the CPU only when it achieves over 95% agreement with the existing model on holdout data, forwarding all other requests to the original LLM. It demonstrated its performance by learning an LLM's movement patterns to run a Snake game at 10 moves per second, an operation impossible with the standalone model. Inside Amazon’s robotic arms race and what it means for the future of human work This article explores how Amazon is accelerating automation by deploying more than 1 million robots in its fulfillment centers, and examines the impact of this shift on human workers' work environments and the future of jobs. Amazon operates the world's largest fleet of warehouse robots, deploying more than 1 million units, with plans to significantly expand robotic deployments in the future. While Amazon executives argue that robotics relieve workers from high-intensity tasks with high risks of fatigue and injury, labor groups and researchers point out that AI-driven automation is not solely beneficial for workers. Unlike in the past, the rise of collaborative robots working in physical proximity to humans is altering workplace dynamics, including allowing robots to dictate the pace of work. Learning to Play Like a Pro: Adaptive Motion Planning and Tracking for Humanoid Robots A white paper on an adaptive motion planning and tracking framework that enables humanoid robots to maintain the athletic motion style of professional athletes while operating stably in real-world environments. Separated the high-level planner and low-level tracker to address the trade-off between professional athlete-style motion and highly reliable task execution. Covers techniques for reconstructing and physically correcting motions extracted from broadcast videos and motion capture data into reference motions that humanoid robots can safely track. Improved robustness on physical hardware to bridge the sim-to-real gap by applying randomized execution speed-based tracker training and a motion speed adapter. Spotify More Than Doubled Monthly Code Changes as AI Accelerated Development As Spotify expanded its adoption of AI-assisted development and more than doubled its monthly code changes, it is revamping its quality and reliability control mechanisms in response. Through AI-assisted development, Spotify more than doubled its monthly merged code changes year-over-year from 8,100 to 17,000. By combining 'Honk'—a background coding agent powered by Claude via the Agent SDK running in Kubernetes pods—with Fleet Management, Spotify is automating complex code modifications and large-scale migrations. As the surge in code changes outpaced validation control capacity, Spotify is strengthening its quality control systems, including monitoring, rollback capabilities, testing, and deployment safeguards. Article: When Should You Use Liquid Clustering? Benchmarking Lakehouse Layout Strategies Benchmarks performance and cost trade-offs among partitioning, Z-Order, and Liquid Clustering according to workload types in lakehouse analytics systems, and presents optimal data layout strategies. In environments with diverse or continuously changing query patterns, Liquid Clustering is recommended as the default option because it eliminates the burden of manual OPTIMIZE operations and full rewrites. In stable workloads where top queries precisely match two to three fixed filter columns, Z-Order delivers optimal file pruning performance and can be up to 5 times faster than Liquid Clustering. In Z-Order multi-column clustering, when a date column is assigned as the second or third priority key, sequential dates become dispersed, leading to a hidden flaw where time-range query performance degrades by 78% compared to simple date partitioning. Presentation: You Can't Trust Your AI Bill. And Neither Can I An analysis showing that expenses from AI adoption are surpassing traditional cloud billing, causing a new cost crisis across software engineering. Rising AI spending is cannibalizing traditional cloud costs, triggering engineering and financial crises. Developers' everyday tool choices and workflows have transformed directly into purchasing and cost-generating decisions. The need to maximize token consumption and manage unit economics is growing, with projections of full autonomous AI adoption by 2028. Bring Your Own Agent: Your Employees' Assistants Are Already Logged In This article covers the emerging security and identity management threats that arise when employees connect personal AI agents to internal corporate systems without authorization, along with potential countermeasures. With the proliferation of personal AI agents, the Bring-Your-Own-Agent phenomenon—where employees connect agents to work systems without formal approval processes—is accelerating. Unlike traditional BYOD, agents are difficult to control via device registration; instead, they directly access and act within internal systems by utilizing employee identities and credentials (such as OAuth, saved passwords, and API keys). Rather than outright blocking agents, organizations need an approach that builds an identity-based control framework to enable safe adoption. Critical SonicWall SMA 1000 Flaw Targeted in 135 Exploitation Attempts After Patch A critical security flaw in SonicWall SMA 1000 appliances is being actively targeted by attackers, facing 135 exploitation attempts just days after a patch was released. Active exploitation attempts by attackers were detected immediately following the disclosure of an unauthenticated SSRF vulnerability (CVE-2026-102255, CVSS 10.0) in the SonicWall SMA 1000 series. Although an emergency patch was deployed on October 6, 2026, a total of 135 attack attempts were observed starting two days later on October 8 via two US-based IP addresses. This product family also suffered attacks last June via zero-day vulnerability exploitation, in which Java webshells and KNUCKLEBALL malware were distributed to steal credentials and compromise internal networks. Attackers Exploit CVSS 10.0 Bricksforge WordPress Flaw Enabling Unauthenticated RCE A critical vulnerability with a severity score of 10.0 enabling unauthenticated remote code execution (RCE) was discovered in the WordPress plugin Bricksforge and is being actively exploited in the wild. The vulnerability CVE-2026-85097 (CVSS 10.0) allows unauthenticated attackers to upload and execute arbitrary PHP code, taking full control of the site. Attackers upload a GIF/PHP polyglot file to bypass MIME type validation during file upload, and then exploit the lack of URL field validation during form submission to trigger RCE. This vulnerability affects Bricksforge versions 3.1.8.9 and earlier, and has been patched in the latest version, 3.1.8.10. CyberXero Uses Claude Code in Campaign That Stole Over 628,000 Ukrainian Records The Russian-nexus threat group CyberXero exploited Anthropic's Claude Code and the open-source AI agent PentAGI to target critical Ukrainian infrastructure and global websites, stealing over 628,000 personal records. CyberXero operated two parallel tracks simultaneously: automated attacks targeting global WordPress and e-commerce websites, and precise manual attacks targeting Ukrainian energy and critical infrastructure. The attackers established a Claude Code cluster of 51 specialized agents integrated with PentAGI and Cobalt Strike to automate reconnaissance, vulnerability exploitation, and payload generation. To circumvent AI safety controls, they utilized prompt context engineering framed as 'authorized penetration testing on internal infrastructure' along with session reset techniques that initiated new sessions upon receiving refusals. \"Draw a working 3D machine with a single prompt\"...'Anatomy' released for Claude Code 'Anatomy', an open-source skill for Claude Code that converts technical concepts based on a user's single-line idea into directly interactive 3D and visualizable code, has been unveiled. Released by developer Ryan, 'Anatomy' is an open-source agent skill that implements abstract technical concepts and complex operating principles of mechanical devices into SVG-based isometric figures and WebGL 3D visualizations. Examples of creations such as a mechanical watch, a Rolls-Royce turbofan engine, and a SpaceX Raptor 2 engine were showcased; the mechanical watch took approximately 2 hours, while the complex engines took over 10 hours of AI code generation, review, and revision. Distributed under the MIT license, it can be utilized in Claude Code, Codex, OpenCode, and others, and supports website embedding via a separate library (anatomy-figures) offering 52 interactive figures. Ai2 Unveils Open-Source Framework 'OLMo Core 3', Boosting MoE Training Efficiency by 2.7x The Allen Institute for AI (Ai2) has released 'OLMo Core 3', an open-source framework that significantly improves the training efficiency and throughput of MoE models. By adopting a DDP-based system instead of the traditional FSDP and keeping experts resident on GPUs, it reduced communication overhead and the burden of all-gather weight operations. When testing a 47-billion-parameter MoE model on an 8x NVIDIA B300 GPU setup, it achieved a throughput of 52,000 tokens per second, roughly 2.7 times higher than before. It combined MXFP8 low-precision arithmetic and row-wise expert parallelism to reduce memory usage and further enhance throughput, making all code and technical reports fully available on GitHub. Sakana AI Detects 73% of Errors in Key Paper Claims Using LLM-Based Peer Review System Sakana AI has unveiled 'MLR', an LLM-based multi-layered peer review system that detects over 73% of key claim errors and logical contradictions in academic papers, along with a new contradiction benchmark. Sakana AI researchers introduced 'MLR', a multi-layered review system that verifies logical errors in papers using a benchmark containing artificially inserted contradictions. MLR detected 73.43% of errors inserted into key claims across 4 review passes (60.79% in a single review), achieving approximately 5 times higher performance compared to the existing AgentReview (14.81%). Designed with a multi-layered structure based on commercial APIs—including an appendix agent, related work review agent, and meta-review agent—it costs approximately $0.47 per review. OrcaRouter Unveils Cybersecurity AI Model Supporting 1 Million-Token Context Window OrcaRouter has unveiled 'OrcaCyber Zero 1.5,' a cybersecurity-specialized AI model based on a 1-million-token context window that analyzes vast amounts of code and supports both vulnerability detection and remediation. Supports a massive 1-million-token context window, enabling single-session analysis of complex attack surfaces and large codebases Goes beyond vulnerability detection to support attack path reasoning, real exploitability verification, and autonomous security agent integration (native function calling and structured outputs) Achieved 100% on Cybench, 95.8% on CVE-Bench, and 93.9% on HumanEval+ in self-evaluations on cybersecurity benchmarks Provided as an OpenAI-compatible hosted API, with access strictly restricted to pre-vetted authorized personnel such as security researchers and red teams Even Gaming and Delivery Giants Join the Fray... China's AI Model Race Heats Up Again Major Chinese service companies, including game developer miHoYo and food delivery platform Meituan, are jumping into developing their own in-house AI models, reigniting a fierce multi-party competition. miHoYo plans to invest up to 100 billion yuan over three years and is moving to secure Nvidia Blackwell chips, while Meituan is actively building in-house LLMs, such as unveiling 'LongCat 2.0'. Xiaomi is integrating its 'MiLM' model with hardware and smart vehicles, favoring proprietary development to reduce reliance on external providers and navigate competitive dynamics with established tech rivals like Alibaba and Tencent. Although distillation techniques have lowered barriers to entry, consolidation via M&A remains sluggish, shifting the focus of the model race from raw benchmark performance to demonstrating real-world business value and cost efficiency. OpenAI Lists Life Sciences Model 'GPT-Rosalind Discovery' on Pricing Table OpenAI has quietly listed a new reasoning model for life sciences and drug discovery, 'GPT-Rosalind Discovery', on its API pricing table without a separate announcement. 'gpt-rosalind-discovery' was added without a separate notice under the life sciences model section of OpenAI's API pricing table. Pricing is set at $5 per 1 million input tokens, $0.50 for cached inputs, and $25 for outputs, identical to existing research models. GPT-Rosalind is a model designed for biology and drug discovery, with the latest version based on GPT-5.5 and made available on a limited basis to qualified institutions. Three Undergraduates from Korea University and Ajou University Reduce Acoustic Trap Computation Time by 2,900x An undergraduate research team from Korea University and Ajou University has developed a mechanical computation technique that reduces the computing time of ultrasonic traps—used to levitate and manipulate objects mid-air using sound waves—by 2,900 times compared to conventional methods. The computational time required to levitate and move objects using ultrasound was reduced by 2,900 times, dropping from 28.93 seconds to 0.01 seconds. Instead of adjusting the acoustic field profile each time, the team introduced a mechanical analysis approach that directly calculates the required forces and optimizes the cross-section of the levitated object. Computer simulations showed an average error of approximately 0.029 mm from the target position, improving precision by about ninefold compared to existing methods and opening applications for microscopic sample imaging and precision parts assembly."